Here are my findings for implementing the "create owner" REST endpoint changes.

## 1. POST YAML that creates an owner

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`

```yaml
composition:
  authorize: "hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')"

build:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.BuildOwner
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: checkTelephone

checkTelephone:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: checkHousehold

checkHousehold:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: checkCityCapacity

checkCityCapacity:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.CheckOwnerCityCapacity
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: checkDailyLimit

checkDailyLimit:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.CheckOwnerDailyLimit
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: countNamesakes

countNamesakes:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.CountOwnerNamesakes
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: save

save:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.SaveOwner
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: respond

respond:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.RespondWithOwnerCreated
  govern: [ transaction ]
```

The pipeline is a linear chain of OfficeFloor functions: `build -> checkTelephone -> checkHousehold -> checkCityCapacity -> checkDailyLimit -> countNamesakes -> save -> respond`. Each function's `service(...)` method is injected with dependencies; `@Val Owner` / `Out<Owner>` variables pass state between them. `RespondWithOwnerCreated` is the terminal step (a natural place to add an AUDIT log line after the owner is persisted and has an id).

## 2. Java function classes referenced in the YAML

All under directory `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/`.

**BuildOwner.java** — builds the Owner from the request DTO, normalizes fields, defaults/rolls `registrationDate` to a business day, and assigns `customerCode` via `nextCustomerCode(...)`. Emits `Out<Owner> built` and `Out<Boolean> sharesHousehold`. Full source shown above (path: `.../owner/BuildOwner.java`). Key lines:
```java
owner.setRegistrationDate(toBusinessDay(registrationDate));
owner.setCustomerCode(nextCustomerCode(owner.getCity(), owner.getLastName(), ownerRepository));
built.set(owner);
```

**CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique.java** — `service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`, throws `DuplicateTelephoneException` (409).

**CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique.java** — `service(@Val Owner owner, @Val Boolean sharesHousehold, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`, throws `DuplicateHouseholdException`; assigns `householdId`.

**CheckOwnerCityCapacity.java** — `service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`, `CAPACITY = 50`, throws `CityAtCapacityException`.

**CheckOwnerDailyLimit.java** — `service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`, `DAILY_LIMIT = 100`, throws `DailyOwnerLimitException` (429). Counts against `owner.getRegistrationDate()`.

**CountOwnerNamesakes.java** — `service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`, sets `owner.setNamesakeCount(count)`.

**SaveOwner.java** (full):
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import net.officefloor.plugin.variable.Val;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.repository.OwnerRepository;

public class SaveOwner {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
        ownerRepository.save(owner);
    }
}
```

**RespondWithOwnerCreated.java** (full) — terminal responder; after this runs `owner.getId()` is populated (post-save):
```java
package org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner;

import java.net.URI;

import net.officefloor.plugin.variable.Val;
import net.officefloor.web.ObjectResponse;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.mapper.OwnerMapper;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.model.Owner;
import org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto;

public class RespondWithOwnerCreated {

    public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerMapper ownerMapper,
            ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<OwnerDto>> response) {
        OwnerDto dto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);
        response.send(ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/owners/" + owner.getId())).body(dto));
    }
}
```
Full source of the four Check* and Count classes is quoted above in the exploration; each takes `@Val Owner owner` plus `OwnerRepository`.

## 3. Owner entity

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (extends `Person`, which holds `id`/`firstName`/`lastName`).

Relevant fields and accessors:
```java
@Column(name = "registration_date", columnDefinition = "DATE")
private java.time.LocalDate registrationDate;

@Column(name = "customer_code")
private String customerCode;

public java.time.LocalDate getRegistrationDate() { return this.registrationDate; }
public String getCustomerCode() { return this.customerCode; }
```
The id getter is `getId()` (inherited from `Person` / `BaseEntity`; used as `owner.getId()` in `RespondWithOwnerCreated` and referenced via `this.getId()` in `Owner.toString()`). Note `registrationDate` is a `java.time.LocalDate` (its `toString()` gives ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`, matching the test's `registrationDate` text assertion).

## 4. Existing tests that POST to owners

The acceptance suite is the relevant harness. All under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/`.

**AcceptanceTest.java** — this is the audit test already present (currently the only concrete acceptance test shown). Path `.../acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`:
```java
/** audit-create: on successful create, an AUDIT log line carries the owner id, the
 * customerCode and the registrationDate. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreLogsAuditOnCreate() throws Exception {
		try (AuditLogCapture audit = new AuditLogCapture()) {
			int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
			JsonNode o = fetchOwner(id);
			assertTrue(audit.anyContains(String.valueOf(id),
					o.get("customerCode").asText(), o.get("registrationDate").asText()));
		}
	}
}
```

**AcceptanceBase.java** — black-box base. POST helpers: `createOwner(body)` → `POST /api/owners`; `createOwnerOk(body)` asserts 2xx and returns the id (via body `id` or `Location` header); `fetchOwner(id)` → `GET /api/owners/{id}`. Runs under `@SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Transactional @WithMockUser(username="acceptance-admin", roles={"OWNER_ADMIN","VET_ADMIN","ADMIN"})`. `ownerNode()` produces a fully-valid unique owner (firstName/lastName/address/city/telephone). Path `.../acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` (full source quoted above).

**AuditLogCapture.java** — the mechanism the audit test uses. It attaches a Logback `ListAppender` to the logger named **`"AUDIT"`** and exposes `anyContains(...)`, `anyAtLevel(...)`, `messages()`, `count()`. Path `.../acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` (full source above). Critically: it captures via `ch.qos.logback.classic.Logger` obtained from `org.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT")` — so the implementation must log to an SLF4J logger literally named `AUDIT`, and the message must contain the owner id, customerCode, and registrationDate substrings.

## 5. Existing "AUDIT" logger usage

There is **no** production/main-code usage of an `AUDIT` logger yet. `grep -rn "AUDIT"` across the repo matches only in tests:
- `.../acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (the audit test + comment)
- `.../acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` (defines `this("AUDIT")` and `LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT")`)

So to satisfy the test you must introduce a new logger `LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT")` in a create-owner function (naturally `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, which has the persisted owner with its id) and log id + customerCode + registrationDate.

## 6. Build file / logging framework / running tests

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml`. Maven wrapper present: `/home/daniel/sandbox/mvnw` (and `mvnw.cmd`).

- Parent: `spring-boot-starter-parent` 4.1.0 (project version 4.0.2). This means the **default logging stack is SLF4J + Logback** (`spring-boot-starter-logging`, pulled transitively by the starters). No explicit log4j/JUL config.
- `spring-boot-starter-test`, `spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test`, and `spring-boot-starter-security-test` are on the test classpath (MockMvc + `@WithMockUser`).
- Logback is directly usable in tests (AuditLogCapture imports `ch.qos.logback.classic.*` and `ch.qos.logback.core.read.ListAppender`), consistent with the Logback backend.
- Existing SLF4J usage pattern to mirror: `/home/daniel/sandbox/.../rest/escalation/GeneralExceptionHandler.java` uses `private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(...)` (`org.slf4j.Logger` / `org.slf4j.LoggerFactory`). For the AUDIT logger use `LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT")` (string name, not a class).

Run the test suite:
- All tests: `./mvnw test` (from `/home/daniel/sandbox`)
- Just the acceptance audit test: `./mvnw test -Dtest=AcceptanceTest`
- Note the build compiles OpenAPI-generated sources (`target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java`) and enforces a minimum Java version via the enforcer plugin, so a full `./mvnw test` (not a bare `javac`) is required for the DTOs to exist.

## 7. Response DTO for owner and `from(owner)`

There is **no** `OwnerResponse` class and **no** `from(owner)` factory method in this codebase. The response DTO is `OwnerDto`, which is **generated** by the OpenAPI generator from `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (schema `Owner`, lines ~2064+) into `target/generated-sources/openapi/...` — that's why no `OwnerDto.java` exists under `src/`.

Mapping from entity to DTO is done by MapStruct, not a `from(...)` method:

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`
```java
@Mapper(uses = PetMapper.class)
public interface OwnerMapper {
    @Mapping(target = "displayName", expression = "java(owner.getLastName() + \", \" + owner.getFirstName())")
    @Mapping(target = "initials", expression = "...")
    @Mapping(target = "membershipNumber",
        expression = "java(owner.getCustomerCode() == null || owner.getRegistrationDate() == null ? null : owner.getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + String.format(\"%02d\", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100))")
    @Mapping(target = "membershipTier", expression = "...")
    @Mapping(target = "locality", expression = "java(...Locality.of(owner.getCity()))")
    OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner);
    ...
}
```

So the DTO returned by the endpoint is `OwnerDto` produced via `ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner)` (called in `RespondWithOwnerCreated`). The OpenAPI `Owner` schema (`.../openapi.yml`) defines the serialized fields, including read-only `customerCode` (`<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) and inherited `registrationDate` (ISO date, from `OwnerFields`), plus `id`, `displayName`, `initials`, `locality`, `membershipNumber`, `householdId`. These are the exact JSON field names (`customerCode`, `registrationDate`, `id`) the acceptance test reads back.

### Implementation summary for the audit requirement
To make `AcceptanceTest.coreLogsAuditOnCreate` pass, add an SLF4J logger named `"AUDIT"` (via `LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT")`) in `RespondWithOwnerCreated.service(...)` (owner id is populated after `SaveOwner`), and log a message that contains all three substrings: `owner.getId()`, `owner.getCustomerCode()`, and `owner.getRegistrationDate()` (its `LocalDate.toString()` yields the ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` the test compares against `o.get("registrationDate").asText()`). The Logback backend (Spring Boot default) is what `AuditLogCapture` hooks into, so no extra logging dependency is needed.

